Scene Description in RDF Triples (Turtle Format)
```turtle
@prefix : <http://cecs571.csulb.edu/ontology/cornercase#> .
@prefix xsd: <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .

:scene1 a :Scene ;
    :scenarioName "NighttimeStreetWithRollingObject" ;
    :hasCornerCaseType :cc1 .

:cc1 a :EvidenceBasedAnomaly ;
    :hasEvidence :evidence1 ;
    :hasObstacle :obstacle1 ;
    :hasRisk :risk1, :risk2 ;
    :hasWeatherCondition :weather1 .

:evidence1 a :RollingBallIndicator ;
    :indicatesPotentialRisk :risk1 .

:obstacle1 a :RoadObject ;
    :description "Unattended rolling object on road" .

:risk1 a :CollisionRisk ;
    :description "Potential collision with rolling object on road" ;
    :hasSeverityScore :sev1 .

:risk2 a :OcclusionRisk ;
    :description "Occlusion risk due to low visibility of rolling object" ;
    :hasSeverityScore :sev2 .

:weather1 a :NightCondition .

:sev1 a :SeverityScore ;
    :severityValue "0.75"^^xsd:float .

:sev2 a :SeverityScore ;
    :severityValue "0.60"^^xsd:float .
```

Identified Risks for each risk class:

**Risk 1:**
Description: Potential collision with rolling object on road
Severity Score: 0.75

**Risk 2:**
Description: Occlusion risk due to low visibility of rolling object
Severity Score: 0.60

---
The scene is classified as an **EvidenceBasedAnomaly** due to the presence of a rolling object (evidence indicator) on the road. The ontology captures the rolling object as a **RoadObject** under **Obstacle**, and the nighttime condition is classified as **NightCondition**. The risks identified are **CollisionRisk** and **OcclusionRisk**, with severity scores reflecting the potential danger of the rolling object and the reduced visibility in nighttime conditions.